WEBVTT

00:00:00.202 --> 00:00:03.552
A two by two matrix can move a picture in
many ways.

00:00:03.552 --> 00:00:04.802
It can rotate it.

00:00:04.802 --> 00:00:06.077
It can scale it.

00:00:06.077 --> 00:00:07.277
It can shear it.

00:00:07.277 --> 00:00:08.777
But watch the origin.

00:00:08.777 --> 00:00:12.452
It never moves, because A times zero is
always zero.

00:00:12.452 --> 00:00:16.952
So sliding an image, the simplest move of
all, is not a matrix product.

00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:20.244
Give every point a third coordinate equal
to one.

00:00:20.244 --> 00:00:23.357
Now translation is a three by three
matrix.

00:00:23.357 --> 00:00:24.557
Multiply it out:

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:28.782
x plus t x, y plus t y, and the one stays
one.

00:00:28.782 --> 00:00:30.832
The origin finally moves.

00:00:30.832 --> 00:00:33.834
Every transformation here is now a single
matrix,

00:00:33.835 --> 00:00:36.470
and a sequence of them is a single
product.

00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:38.095
Take the point one, one.

00:00:38.095 --> 00:00:41.570
Rotate it ninety degrees and it lands on
minus one, one.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.983
Translate by two, zero, and you end at
one, one.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.092
Now swap them.

00:00:46.092 --> 00:00:48.080
Translate first, then rotate.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.317
Where does the point land?

00:00:49.317 --> 00:00:50.955
Pause here and work it out.

00:00:52.454 --> 00:00:55.154
Translating first gives three, one.

00:00:55.154 --> 00:00:57.641
Rotating that gives minus one, three:

00:00:57.641 --> 00:00:58.829
a different point.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:00.754
Read the product from the right.

00:01:00.754 --> 00:01:03.254
The matrix nearest the point acts first.

00:01:03.257 --> 00:01:06.794
Restrict the entries of the matrix and you
get a family of moves.

00:01:06.794 --> 00:01:10.469
Translation has two numbers, and only
position changes.

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:11.594
Rigid has three:

00:01:11.594 --> 00:01:13.582
lengths and angles survive.

00:01:13.582 --> 00:01:15.094
Similarity has four:

00:01:15.094 --> 00:01:17.282
angles and ratios of lengths.

00:01:17.282 --> 00:01:18.669
Affine has six:

00:01:18.669 --> 00:01:20.844
parallel lines stay parallel.

00:01:20.844 --> 00:01:24.282
Projective has eight, and only straight
lines stay straight.

00:01:24.252 --> 00:01:28.127
After a projective map the last coordinate
is usually not one,

00:01:28.128 --> 00:01:30.003
so read the point by dividing.

00:01:30.002 --> 00:01:33.690
Two, four, two and one, two, one are the
same point:

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.915
every vector on this ray names it.

00:01:35.915 --> 00:01:38.554
As the ray tips over, its point runs away,

00:01:38.555 --> 00:01:42.578
and when the last coordinate is zero there
is no ordinary point.

00:01:42.577 --> 00:01:45.452
It names a direction, a point at infinity.

00:01:45.386 --> 00:01:47.324
A line is a vector too.

00:01:47.324 --> 00:01:52.176
The lines y equals one and y equals two
have the vectors zero, one,

00:01:52.177 --> 00:01:54.712
minus one and zero, one, minus two.

00:01:54.711 --> 00:01:57.936
Their cross product is minus one, zero,
zero.

00:01:57.936 --> 00:01:59.899
Its last coordinate is zero:

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:01.499
a point at infinity.

00:02:01.499 --> 00:02:03.965
A homography can bring that point into
view,

00:02:03.966 --> 00:02:06.712
which is why railway tracks meet in a
photograph.
